<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The WinUI 3 shell. Windows, XAML, key dispatch, platform adapters — and nothing else.

    Everything it draws is decided in astrid-core and arrives as a command response. If a file in
    this project ever needs to know what a row shows, which tasks belong in a list, or what
    completing one means, the answer belongs in the core and this project should be asking for it.
  -->
  <!--
    The words. One file per language under Strings/<tag>/; a translator adds a folder beside en-US
    and nothing else changes — the SDK picks up every .resw in the project on its own. Strings.cs
    holds the same English as a fallback for the build whose .pri does not make it next to the
    executable.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows10.0.19041.0</TargetFramework>
    <TargetPlatformMinVersion>10.0.17763.0</TargetPlatformMinVersion>
    <RootNamespace>Astrid.App</RootNamespace>
    <ApplicationManifest>app.manifest</ApplicationManifest>
    <!--
      The taskbar, Alt-Tab and Explorer read this, and only this — the MSIX tiles do not reach the
      UNPACKAGED build, which is the one a developer runs and the one the smoke tests drive. It had
      no icon at all and wore the generic .NET one. Generated from the shared master by
      scripts/make-icons.ps1 (task 70ffe6c3).
    -->
    <ApplicationIcon>Assets\Astrid.ico</ApplicationIcon>
    <Platforms>x86;x64;ARM64</Platforms>
    <RuntimeIdentifiers>win-x64;win-arm64</RuntimeIdentifiers>
    <UseWinUI>true</UseWinUI>
    <!--
      Unpackaged by default, packaged on request.

      The unpackaged build is what a developer runs with F5 and what the UI smoke tests drive, so
      it stays the default. `-p:AstridPackaged=true` is the Store build, and the only one whose
      `astrid://` comes from the manifest rather than from the app writing its own registry key at
      startup.

      WindowsPackageType IS None FOR BOTH, and the second property below is why.

      Leaving it unset is not the SDK's way of saying MSIX — this comment used to say it was, and
      that cost every MSIX this repo ever built (task 76fcd157).
      Microsoft.Windows.SDK.BuildTools.MSIX defaults an empty WindowsPackageType to MSIX only when
      a `Package.appxmanifest` sits beside the project, and otherwise to None. This project has no
      such file on purpose: the manifest is hand-written at packaging/AppxManifest.xml with
      {VERSION}/{ARCHITECTURE} placeholders that package.ps1 substitutes, so the csproj must not be
      what packages it. Saying MSIX here is worse than useless — the tooling then demands an
      @(AppxManifest) item and fails the publish outright.

      But None also turns on WindowsAppSdkBootstrapInitialize
      (Microsoft.WindowsAppSDK.BootstrapCommon.targets), which compiles the Windows App SDK
      bootstrapper's auto-initializer into the exe. The bootstrapper is how an app WITHOUT package
      identity finds the runtime; called from inside an MSIX it returns 0x80070032
      ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED and the initializer fails fast, so the packaged app died before the first
      line of Main. Installing worked and launching never did — the same trap as the
      PackageDependency this manifest was missing until 2026-10-18, one layer further down, and
      invisible to F5 and the UI smoke tests because they drive the unpackaged build, where the
      bootstrapper is both correct and necessary.

      scripts/tests/packaging-manifest.Tests.ps1 holds both halves to MSBuild's EVALUATED output,
      because the broken version of this comment read as correct.

      One property rather than a second project: a .wapproj would mean a second build and a second
      place for the version number to be wrong, which is the reason the manifest is hand-written
      too (see packaging/README.md).
    -->
    <WindowsPackageType Condition="'$(AstridPackaged)' != 'true'">None</WindowsPackageType>
    <WindowsAppSdkBootstrapInitialize Condition="'$(AstridPackaged)' == 'true'">false</WindowsAppSdkBootstrapInitialize>
    <EnableMsixTooling>true</EnableMsixTooling>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <!--
      LibraryImport generates unsafe marshalling code, the same as the bindings project. The app's
      own code has no unsafe blocks in it.
    -->
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <!--
      The entry point is hand-written (Program.cs) so the app can be single-instance and receive
      the sign-in callback as a redirected activation. The generated one starts a second copy per
      launch, which for this app means a second copy with none of the flow state the first is
      waiting with.
    -->
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);DISABLE_XAML_GENERATED_MAIN</DefineConstants>
    <!--
      Compile the IL ahead of time in the published build (task d24777f5).

      Only `dotnet publish -r <rid>` can do this, which is exactly what scripts/package.ps1 runs
      per architecture, so it costs a developer's inner loop nothing: `dotnet build` and F5 are
      untouched. What it buys is the first screen. Without it the JIT compiles the startup path —
      the generated XAML, the view models, the bindings — while the user is looking at the window,
      and on an ARM64 machine that is the slowest work the app ever does.

      ReadyToRun is a cache, never a replacement: the IL ships beside the native code and the
      runtime falls back to it whenever the precompiled form does not apply. So nothing here can
      change behaviour, only the package's size, which is the trade being made.

      NOT PublishTrimmed, deliberately, though the same review asked for both. WinUI resolves XAML
      types by name at run time — a page is found through its generated type name, bindings through
      reflection over the view model — and the trimmer cannot see any of that. It would be a
      packaging flag whose failure mode is a blank page in a signed build that every test here
      passes, since the tests drive the unpackaged build and never publish. Worth doing one day
      behind the UI smoke suite running against a trimmed publish; not worth doing blind.
    -->
    <PublishReadyToRun>true</PublishReadyToRun>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.WindowsAppSDK" Version="2.7.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Windows.SDK.BuildTools" Version="10.0.28000.2957" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The shared checkbox art, vendored at design/AstridCheckbox.xaml.

    Linked rather than copied, so there is one file in the repo and `DesignTokenTests` gates the
    same bytes the shell compiles: the vendored copy carries a `body-sha256` of its own body, and a
    second copy here could drift from it silently. astrid-core generates it from the source the web
    and the Apple clients draw, and it strokes from `{TemplateBinding Foreground}` so a priority
    token can colour it (task 82e4ad32, DS21).

    It replaced the sixteen PNGs that used to live beside it, which baked priority, repetition and
    completion into one picture — and four colours in with them. Those files are no longer
    referenced; removing them needs an approval this loop does not have, so they are left in place.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Page Include="..\..\design\AstridCheckbox.xaml" Link="Assets\Design\AstridCheckbox.xaml" />
    <!--
      The generated token dictionary, vendored at design/AstridTokens.xaml and linked for the same
      reason: `App.xaml` merges it instead of hand-writing sixty-six brushes, so the colours the
      shell paints with ARE astrid-core's output rather than a copy checked against it (task
      1fae8c54).
    -->
    <Page Include="..\..\design\AstridTokens.xaml" Link="Assets\Design\AstridTokens.xaml" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The mark the window draws — title bar, sign-in, the empty list, a reminder — at the web's
      two sizes (BRAND.icon 512, BRAND.iconSmall 96). Written by scripts/make-icons.ps1 from the
      same master as the taskbar icon and the Store tiles.
    -->
    <Content Include="Assets\Astrid-*.png">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
    <!--
      And the .ico beside them. It is already compiled into the executable by <ApplicationIcon>
      above, which is what Explorer and the taskbar read — but AppWindow.SetIcon, which is the only
      thing that reaches the title bar and Alt-Tab, wants a file (task 28348df8).
    -->
    <Content Include="Assets\Astrid.ico">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Astrid.Core.Bindings\Astrid.Core.Bindings.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Astrid.App.ViewModels\Astrid.App.ViewModels.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
